{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And he blessed Joseph.</b> We see no blessing for Joseph here. And Ramban explained that the blessing of the sons is the blessing of Joseph, and so forth. But the mind is not at ease with this, for why should Joseph himself not be blessed in matters that pertain to him specifically? And what seems correct in my eyes is that in the word ״וַיְבָרֶךְ״ the verse intimated that he blessed Joseph that he would be continually blessed." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained according to their words, as they said (Genesis Rabbah 39:11), that the key of blessings was in the hand of Abraham, and he handed it to Isaac, and Isaac handed it to Jacob (Genesis Rabbah 61), and Jacob handed it to Joseph. And this is the meaning of His saying <b>and he blessed</b> — and so forth — meaning, he handed into his hand the key of blessings." "<b>And he said: The Elohim, and so forth.</b> He began to arouse the love of the ancestors in the order of the prayer of the Eighteen Benedictions that they pray, which they begin with the remembrance of the fathers, and he said: ״The Elohim who, and so forth, Abraham and Isaac,״ and he also mentioned his own merit in modesty, saying ״The Elohim who has been my shepherd,״ and here he alluded to his merit on this path, for he, Jacob, would walk of his own accord, as it were, before the Shepherd who was shepherding him. And after he arranged the merit of the fathers, he began to pray: ״The angel,״ and so forth."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:50:06.971969209Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}